Z||000006||Mach10||08-21-2001||03:28 PM||||I drive mine in the winter. Well, I havent driven MINE in the winter yet, but I have driven v6 Fieros in snow and on ice. They aren't as forgiving as FWD or AWD cars, but if treated nice, they are predictable, and have good starting-stopping-turning characteristics. After an ice storm, I don't even WALK to school, so don't ask  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000007||onfire||08-23-2001||03:51 PM||||Fiero's are great in the snow. This is the first Fiero I ever owned, it was an 85 GT. It was my daily driver for 2 and a half years. I never had any problems in the snow or the rain, and I live where it rains 9 months out of the year.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> This picture was taken in North Bend, WA going up some logging roads. It got really deep after about 2 miles. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/snow1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
